menu "epet configuration"

    config EPET_DISPLAY_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Display blank timeout (ms)"
        default 30000
        range 0 3600000
        help
            Blank the LCD after this many milliseconds with no button
            activity. The pet keeps living while the panel sleeps; only the
            display is powered down. A button press wakes the display and is
            swallowed, so it does not also trigger an action.

            Set to 0 to keep the display on permanently.

    config EPET_LIGHT_SLEEP
        bool "Enter light sleep while the display is blanked"
        default y
        help
            Halt the CPU with esp_light_sleep_start() while the panel is off,
            waking on a button GPIO going low. Cuts idle current by roughly an
            order of magnitude versus simply polling.

            The pet is not paused: esp_timer is compensated across light
            sleep, so the elapsed time is replayed through epet_advance() on
            wake and hunger/age catch up correctly.

            Disable to fall back to polling at EPET_IDLE_POLL_MS.

    config EPET_WAKE_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Periodic wake interval while asleep (ms, 0 = GPIO only)"
        default 60000
        range 0 3600000
        help
            While in light sleep, also wake on a timer this often to advance
            the simulation and let modules react -- poops appear, needs cross
            their thresholds, and the care module can ask for attention.

            The display is NOT turned on by a timer wake; only a button press
            or an explicit alert does that. Set to 0 for GPIO-only wake, in
            which case nothing happens until the user presses something.

    config EPET_MENU_HIDE_MS
        int "Hide the menu overlay after this long unused (ms, 0 = never)"
        default 5000
        range 0 600000
        help
            The menu floats over the scene and fades out when unused, leaving
            the pet unobstructed. LT or LB brings it back; that reveal press
            is consumed, so it never also moves the cursor or opens a page.

    config EPET_AUTOSAVE_MS
        int "Minimum gap between automatic saves (ms)"
        default 30000
        range 1000 3600000
        help
            The pet is written to NVS at most this often, and only when
            something actually changed. It is also flushed just before
            entering light sleep, since the next wake may be minutes away.

    config EPET_ALERT_SHOW_MS
        int "How long an attention alert lights the screen (ms)"
        default 4000
        range 500 60000

    config EPET_CARE_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Minimum gap between attention alerts (ms)"
        default 120000
        range 1000 3600000
        help
            Rate limit so an unmet need nags at most this often.

    config EPET_IDLE_POLL_MS
        int "Button poll interval while display is asleep (ms)"
        default 120
        range 20 1000
        help
            Used only when EPET_LIGHT_SLEEP is disabled. While the panel is
            blanked there is nothing to draw, so the main loop slows to this
            interval instead of the ~33 ms render cadence.

    config EPET_SHAKE_WAKE
        bool "Wake the display when the device is shaken"
        default y
        help
            Reads the onboard QMI8658 accelerometer and lights the screen
            when the pet is picked up or shaken.

    config EPET_SHAKE_THRESHOLD_MG
        int "Shake threshold (milli-g of change between samples)"
        default 300
        range 80 2000
        depends on EPET_SHAKE_WAKE
        help
            How much the accelerometer must change between two samples.
            At rest this reads a few mg; a deliberate shake reads several
            hundred. Lower is twitchier.

    config EPET_SHAKE_HOLD_MS
        int "How long the shaking must continue to count (ms)"
        default 1000
        range 0 10000
        depends on EPET_SHAKE_WAKE
        help
            A single knock should not wake the pet. Movement has to keep
            exceeding the threshold for roughly this long before the screen
            lights. Credit builds while the board is moving and drains at
            half rate while it is still, so brief pauses mid-shake do not
            reset it but a one-off bump fades away.

            Set to 0 to wake on the first qualifying movement.

    config EPET_REVIVE_HOLD_MS
        int "How long to shake a dead pet to start a new one (ms)"
        default 5000
        range 0 30000
        depends on EPET_SHAKE_WAKE
        help
            Longer than the wake hold: ending a pet's afterlife and rolling a
            new class should take more than an accidental jostle. The screen
            lights at the normal wake hold so there is visible feedback that
            shaking is doing something, and the new pet arrives at this one.

    config EPET_SHAKE_CONFIRM_MS
        int "Poll interval while a possible shake is in progress (ms)"
        default 120
        range 40 1000
        depends on EPET_SHAKE_WAKE
        help
            Once movement is seen, sampling speeds up to judge whether it is
            sustained. Polling at the idle rate would need several seconds to
            collect enough samples, and would miss the gaps in a real shake.

    config EPET_SHAKE_POLL_MS
        int "How often to check for movement while asleep (ms)"
        default 400
        range 100 5000
        depends on EPET_SHAKE_WAKE
        help
            While the screen is blanked the CPU is in light sleep, so the
            accelerometer can only be read on a timer wake. This is the
            responsiveness/battery trade: each wake is a few milliseconds of
            CPU plus one I2C read.

            Set EPET_IMU_INT_GPIO instead if you know which pin the IMU's
            interrupt line reaches; that wakes on motion with no polling.

    config EPET_IMU_INT_GPIO
        int "IMU interrupt GPIO (-1 = poll instead)"
        default -1
        range -1 48
        depends on EPET_SHAKE_WAKE
        help
            The schematic names IMU_INT1/IMU_INT2 but does not say which
            GPIO they reach, and the vendor header is wrong about this part
            elsewhere, so this is not set by default. If you confirm the pin,
            set it here: the device then wakes on the IMU's own motion
            interrupt and does no polling at all.

    config EPET_IMU_SCAN
        bool "Scan for the IMU if it is not where expected"
        default n
        depends on EPET_SHAKE_WAKE
        help
            Probes candidate SDA/SCL pin pairs at boot and logs any I2C
            device found. Diagnostic: turn it off once the pins are known.

    config EPET_BLE
        bool "Expose the module link over Bluetooth LE"
        default y
        help
            Advertises a Nordic UART Service carrying the same '#' line
            protocol as the serial link, so a browser can install modules
            over Web Bluetooth instead of Web Serial.

            Costs roughly 200 KB of flash and 40 KB of RAM. The device stays
            awake while a central is connected, since light sleep would stop
            it servicing the link.

    menu "Button GPIOs"
        comment "Buttons are positional: two on each side of the screen."
        comment "Set to -1 for a button that is not wired."

        config EPET_BTN_LT_GPIO
            int "Left top (feed)"
            default 1
            range -1 48

        config EPET_BTN_LB_GPIO
            int "Left bottom (play)"
            default 2
            range -1 48

        config EPET_BTN_RT_GPIO
            int "Right top (clean)"
            default 5
            range -1 48

        config EPET_BTN_RB_GPIO
            int "Right bottom (confirm)"
            default 6
            range -1 48

        config EPET_BOOT_AS_LT
            bool "Also treat the onboard BOOT button (GPIO0) as Left-top"
            default y
            help
                Lets the board be exercised before any buttons are wired.
                Turn this off once real buttons are fitted, or GPIO0 will
                double up as feed.
    endmenu

endmenu
